%I A399970 #10 Sep 30 2026 17:04:24
%S A399970 120,420,528,1008,1520,1680,1980,2080,2380,3432,3480,4095,5460,5852,
%T A399970 6440,6888,7812,8160
%N A399970 Largest elements of integral Diophantine quadruples, among which one plus the product of any two distinct terms is a square.
%C A399970 A tuple of positive integers (b(1), b(2), ..., b(k)) is called an integral Diophantine k-tuple when b(i) * b(j) + 1 with 1 <= i< j <= k are all square.
%C A399970 The first example for an integral Diophantine quadruple (1, 3, 8, 120) was found by Fermat (see A030063).
%C A399970 Arkin, Hoggatt, and Straus found that when (a, b, c) is an integral Diophantine triple, then, putting r^2 = ab+1, s^2 = bc+1, t^2 = ac+1, and d+ = a + b + c + 2abc + 2rst, (a, b, c, d+) is an integral Diophantine quadruple and d+ > max(a, b, c); i.e., d+ belongs to this sequence. Such a quadruple is called regular. It is conjectured that any Diophantine quadruple must be regular.
%C A399970 He, Togbe, and Ziegler proved that there is no Diophantine quintuple.
%D A399970 A. Dujella, Diophantine m-tuples and Elliptic Curves Developments in Mathematics, Springer, 2025.
%D A399970 R. K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, 3rd edition, Springer-Verlag, 2004, D29.
%H A399970 J. Arkin, V. E. Hoggatt Jr., and E. G. Straus, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/17-4/arkin.pdf">On Euler's solution of a problem of Diophantus</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 17 (1979), 333-339.
%H A399970 A. Baker and H. Davenport, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/qmath/20.1.129">The equations 3x^2-2=y^2 and 8x^2-7=z^2</a>, Quart. J. Math. Oxford 20 (1969), 129-137.
%H A399970 A. Dujella, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1515/CRLL.2004.003">There are only finitely many Diophantine quintuples</a>, J. Reine Angew. Math, 566 (2004), 183-214, available from <a href="https://www.academia.edu/130404256/There_are_only_finitely_many_Diophantine_quintuples">Academia</a> and <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228984361_There_are_only_finitely_many_Diophantine_quintuples">ResearchGate</a>.
%H A399970 B. He, A. Togbe, and V. Ziegler, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/tran/7573">There is no Diophantine quintuple</a>, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 371 (2019), 6665-6709.
%H A399970 H. Nakao, <a href="https://www.kaynet.or.jp/~kay/misc/dquadruple.html">Diophantine quadruples (in Japanese)</a>.
%e A399970 120 belongs to this sequence since (1, 3, 8, 120) is an integral Diophantine quadruple (see A030063).
%Y A399970 Cf. A030063 (Fermat's integral Diophantine quadruple).
